# NLS SES / BA-degree coefficient-instability analysis — run summary

## Question
Which variable's effect on BA DEGREE COMPLETED falls from 0.5024 to 0.0923 (and loses significance)
once SES **and** academic characteristics are jointly included?

## Answer
**SAMPLE_RACE** — the racial gap in BA completion shrinks by ~82–99% and becomes non-significant once
SES and the academic block (ASVAB ability composite + class percentile) are jointly included, while SES,
the academic block and family size all stay significant. The collapse is driven by the **academic block**
(above all the ASVAB ability composite), not by SES: SES alone leaves the race gap highly significant.

## Data
data/nls_ses_processed.csv — 8,773 rows x 8 substantive columns (+1 unnamed index column); 0 missing;
BA DEGREE COMPLETED boolean, 236/8773 = 2.69% prevalence; SES ~centred (SD 0.74), ABILITY on a raw
1016–3068 scale, PERCENTILE on a raw 0–100 scale. No curriculum or ASVAB-component columns exist in
the file (dataset description mentions them) -> academic block = z(ABILITY) + z(PERCENTILE) [assumption].

## Method (as approved)
Primary: logistic regression on raw predictors. Nested per focal variable:
M0 focal only | M1 +SES | M2 +ACADEMIC | M3 +SES+ACADEMIC(+family-size control) | M3n (no family size) |
academic variables entered one at a time. Dummies: Black, Hispanic (ref White), Female (ref Male).
Comparability checks: standardised-predictor logit, probit, OLS/LPM (HC3).
Diagnostics: VIF, condition number, LR block tests (1-df/2-df, reference-category free), Firth penalised
logit (fully adjusted model only), average marginal effects, ORs with 95% CI, attenuation-ratio matching
against the reported 0.5024 -> 0.0923 pair.

